Batch work for realisateur (owns the guard family): audit bin/check-project-busy.sh and bin/focus-commit.sh for the same class of bug just fixed in bin/notify-senechal.sh (aebf5f4). That bug was a pipefail/SIGPIPE trap -- under `set -o pipefail`, a `producer | grep -q needle` pipeline returns 141 when grep exits on first match and the producer is still writing, so a SUCCESSFUL match reports failure. It is timing-dependent (only bites once the input is big enough that the producer has not finished), and invisible when tested by hand because interactive shells do not set pipefail. Both scripts are in scope for a real reason, not symmetry: these guards report pass/fail that OTHER agents act on, so a false negative is not a local bug -- it makes every caller distrust or re-do a correct action, and notify-senechal false-negatives specifically cause duplicate notes. focus-commit is the higher priority of the two: it does verification work on a multi-writer file (the sequence it exists to replace has already lost content four times), so a wrong verdict there has the worst blast radius. Suggested sweep, wider than the one bug: (1) every `| grep -q` / `| head -c` / early-exiting consumer under pipefail; (2) any check whose failure path cannot distinguish "genuinely failed" from "could not verify" -- senechal lib/common.sh already has the 0/1/2/3 exit contract for exactly that, and a guard that collapses could-not-check into failure is the same false-negative shape one level up. Fix pattern used in notify-senechal: no pipelines in the check -- read into a variable, match with a here-string, slice with bash parameter expansion instead of `| head -c N`.
